# Project repos nested here keep their own remotes and are not tracked by the
# workbench. Add one line per repo. A path with square brackets in it needs
# them escaped, because git reads [...] as a glob character class.
# /example-app/

# Editor, OS, and interpreter noise
.DS_Store
node_modules/
__pycache__/

# The template ships no .obsidian/ directory, so these four rules match nothing
# on a fresh clone. They are here for the vault you configure yourself, and they
# stop a colleague's per-person state entering a commit once one of you does.

# Obsidian per-person view state: pane layout, scroll position, recent files,
# and the graph view's zoom. Obsidian rewrites these under itself every session
# and nobody reads them. The documentation names the first two: workspace.json
# and workspaces.json "store the current workspace layout and update whenever
# you open a new file" (https://obsidian.md/help/data-storage, retrieved
# 2026-09-11). It documents no list of the other files in the configuration
# folder, so graph.json is here on observation and not on a citation.
.obsidian/workspace*.json
.obsidian/graph.json

# Obsidian plugins and themes are third-party code, reinstallable from inside
# Obsidian, so no copy of either belongs in a commit. community-plugins.json is
# not matched, so a vault that tracks it keeps the list of what to reinstall
# without the code.
.obsidian/plugins/
.obsidian/themes/

# A vault opened on a subdirectory keeps its own configuration folder. Repeat
# the four lines above under that path when you open one.

# Hook output. Nobody reads it.
runs/
